            "One critical design aspect is ensuring that the timescale of your repeated measures matches the timescale of the phenomena you are studying, and ideally you choose measurement timepoints that accurately reflect the underlying pattern of temporal change.",
            "If you are examining change across timepoints, equal spacing between repeated measures typically allows for a more parsimonious analytic model than unequal spacing, though you may have theoretical or practical reasons for unequal spacing.",
            "Researchers may end up with unequal spacing due to the nature of their variables, such as a Monday-through-Friday daily diary study in which weekends can be marked as missing data points, even though weekends are not missing at random and could be meaningfully different.",
            "If your variable of interest is slow-moving, you may want to spread your measurements out over a longer period of time, whereas fast-changing processes like heart rate can be assessed every few seconds and slower ones like salivary cortisol are spaced further apart.",
